Minimum-Dissipation Invariant Reconciliation on Networks: Spectral Geometry, Thermodynamic Control, and Global Consistency

Ouadi Maakoul & SI TEAM


Abstract

Distributed physical, computational, biological, and engineered systems frequently undergo local state transformations while maintaining global constraints such as conservation, consistency, balance, or functional viability. We develop a mathematical framework for studying the minimum dissipation required to reconcile such distributed perturbations across a connected network. A network is represented by a weighted graph G=(V,E) with graph Laplacian L_G=BG_eB^\top. Local reconciliation is represented by a node-space control v(t)\in\mathbf 1^\perp, while the minimum edge-space dissipation required to realize that control is shown to be P_{\min}(v)=v^\top L_G^+v. We establish this identity via both convex duality and linear irreversible thermodynamics, show that the reconciliation dynamics decouple exactly into Laplacian eigenmodes, and derive a closed-form spectral error allocation law that we term the Spectral Water-Filling Principle. The resulting energetic geometry is governed by L_G^+, whereas passive relaxation is governed by L_G. This produces a precise distinction between two network geometries: the Laplacian determines how rapidly disagreement naturally relaxes, while its pseudoinverse determines the energetic metric for actively correcting disagreement. The algebraic connectivity \lambda_2(L_G) controls the slowest relaxation mode and the worst-case energetic susceptibility, while \lambda_N(L_G) determines the least costly spectral direction. We derive exact modal solutions, short-time asymptotics, spectral bounds, and the optimal terminal error allocation. The framework is intended as a mathematical foundation for studying distributed invariant preservation under finite-time physical constraints.

---

1. Introduction

Many systems exhibit a recurring structural problem. A system possesses a global state distributed across multiple interacting components. Local transformations perturb those components. Information, material, energy, obligations, or other conserved quantities then move through a network. Finally, the system must determine whether the resulting distributed state remains globally consistent. Examples occur across physics, biology, computation, communication networks, finance, and engineered control systems.

A useful abstraction is

S_t \xrightarrow{T} M_t \xrightarrow{R_G} M_{t+1} \xrightarrow{C} S_{t+1},

where T denotes local transformation, R_G routing through a network topology G, and C reconciliation into a globally meaningful state.

The present work does not claim that these domains are physically identical. Instead, it asks a narrower mathematical question: Given a distributed state perturbation and a network topology, what is the minimum dissipation required to restore global consistency within a prescribed tolerance and finite time?

This question requires simultaneously specifying the network topology, the passive relaxation dynamics, the admissible control operations, the physical or phenomenological cost of those operations, the invariant that must be preserved, and the terminal consistency requirement.

The central observation is that these ingredients naturally generate two different mathematical geometries. The first is generated by the graph Laplacian L_G, which determines passive relaxation. The second is generated by the pseudoinverse L_G^+, which determines the minimum energetic cost of realizing a distributed correction. We therefore investigate the pair

\boxed{(L_G,\;L_G^+)}

as a coupled description of passive relaxation and active reconciliation.

---

2. Related Mathematical Frameworks

The framework intersects several established areas.

Effective resistance and electrical network theory. The identity P_{\min}(v)=v^\top L_G^+v is the effective-resistance identity from electrical network theory. For a weighted graph with Laplacian Q and Moore–Penrose pseudoinverse Q^\dagger, the effective resistance between vertices a and b is r_{\text{eff}}(a,b)=(e_a-e_b)^\top Q^\dagger(e_a-e_b). Fiedler's identity relates the Laplacian, the resistance matrix, and the geometry of the associated simplex. The minimum electrical energy flow among all flows routing demand \mathbf d is P_{\min}(\mathbf d)=\mathbf d^\top L_G^\dagger\mathbf d.

Laplacian and pseudoinverse Laplacian flows. Recent work establishes that for undirected graphs and weight-balanced digraphs, the Laplacian flow and the pseudoinverse Laplacian flow exhibit an interdependence in terms of consensus: the pseudoinverse Laplacian flow system converges to consensus if and only if the Laplacian flow system achieves consensus. This supports the distinction between relaxation geometry and energetic control geometry.

LQ cost and effective resistance. The performance of the linear consensus algorithm has been studied using a linear-quadratic (LQ) cost, with bounds derived via effective resistance and reversibilization. The LQ performance cost can be rewritten as the average effective resistance on a suitable electrical network.

Thermodynamic optimal control. For chemical reaction networks in the adiabatic limit, minimizing total dissipation leads to a geodesic trajectory in probability space, with the metric tensor depending on network topology and link capacities. The entropy production rate in linear irreversible thermodynamics is T\dot S_i=J_e^\top L_e^{-1}J_e.

Finite-time consensus. Finite-time consensus protocols typically use nonlinear graph Laplacians, such as the (p+1)-Laplacian, which are nonlinear generalizations of the standard Laplacian. The present framework achieves finite-time terminal accuracy via active control with a terminal constraint, not by modifying the graph Laplacian.

Water-filling. The water-filling principle is well established in information theory for optimal power allocation across parallel channels. Its application to the spectral modes of a graph Laplacian is a mathematically consistent adaptation that we develop below; we emphasize the algebraic distinction between the exponential decay factors e^{-\lambda_k\tau} that parametrize the passive baseline and the bounded spectral weights \alpha_k(\tau) that govern active suppression.

---

3. Network Model

Let G=(V,E) be a connected weighted graph with |V|=N, |E|=M. Let B\in\mathbb R^{N\times M} be an oriented incidence matrix and G_e=\operatorname{diag}(g_1,\ldots,g_M), g_e>0, the diagonal matrix of edge conductances.

The weighted graph Laplacian is

\boxed{L_G=BG_eB^\top.}

Because the graph is connected, \ker L_G=\operatorname{span}\{\mathbf1\}, so L_G\mathbf1=0. The vector \mathbf1 represents the globally conserved mode.

---

4. Conserved Invariant and Consistency Measure

Let the distributed state be x(t)\in\mathbb R^N. Define the global invariant I(x)=\mathbf1^\top x and the projection onto the non-conserved subspace

\boxed{P_\perp=I-\frac1N\mathbf1\mathbf1^\top.}

Then x=\bar x\mathbf1+P_\perp x, where \bar x=\frac1N\mathbf1^\top x is the average state. The component P_\perp x represents distributed inconsistency. The globally reconciled state is x^*=\bar x\mathbf1. A natural consistency measure is D(x,x^*)=\|P_\perp x\|_2^2, and the reconciliation requirement is \|P_\perp x(\tau)\|_2^2\le\epsilon.

---

5. Passive Dynamics and Active Reconciliation

We consider the controlled dynamics

\boxed{\dot x(t)=-L_Gx(t)+v(t),}

where v(t)\in\operatorname{Range}(L_G)=\mathbf1^\perp. The term -L_Gx represents passive network relaxation, and v(t) represents active reconciliation. Because \mathbf1^\top L_G=0 and \mathbf1^\top v=0, we obtain \frac{d}{dt}\mathbf1^\top x(t)=0, so

\boxed{I(x(t))=I(x(0))}

for all t. The control therefore modifies consistency without violating the conserved global quantity.

---

6. Edge-Space Dissipation

Suppose the node-space correction is generated by edge currents v=Bu, where u\in\mathbb R^M denotes the vector of edge currents. We define the instantaneous edge-space dissipation functional

P(u)=u^\top G_e^{-1}u.

Under an appropriate linear irreversible-thermodynamic constitutive law, this quadratic form may represent physical entropy-production-related power. In the general mathematical framework, however, it is treated first as a dissipation/control metric. The minimum dissipation required to generate a prescribed node-space correction v is

P_{\min}(v)=\min_{u:Bu=v} u^\top G_e^{-1}u.

---

7. The Edge-to-Node Metric Identity

Theorem 1 (Control Metric Identity). For every v\in\operatorname{Range}(L_G)=\mathbf1^\perp, the minimum edge-space dissipation satisfying Bu=v is

\boxed{P_{\min}(v)=v^\top L_G^+v.}

Proof. Consider \mathcal L(u,\gamma)=u^\top G_e^{-1}u+\gamma^\top(v-Bu). Stationarity with respect to u gives 2G_e^{-1}u-B^\top\gamma=0, hence u=\frac12G_eB^\top\gamma. Substitution into Bu=v gives \frac12BG_eB^\top\gamma=v, or \frac12L_G\gamma=v. Since v\in\mathbf1^\perp, \gamma=2L_G^+v is the minimum-norm solution. Hence u^*=G_eB^\top L_G^+v. Evaluating the dissipation,

\begin{aligned}
P_{\min}(v) &= u^{*\top}G_e^{-1}u^*\\
&= v^\top L_G^+BG_eB^\top L_G^+v\\
&= v^\top L_G^+L_GL_G^+v.
\end{aligned}

Using L_G^+L_GL_G^+=L_G^+, we obtain P_{\min}(v)=v^\top L_G^+v. \square

This is the effective-resistance identity from electrical network theory. Under the constitutive identification J_e=L_eF_e, the entropy production rate satisfies T\dot S_i=J_e^\top L_e^{-1}J_e. Under the correspondence L_e=G_e, the edge-space quadratic form becomes T\dot S_i=u^\top G_e^{-1}u, providing a thermodynamic interpretation under specified physical assumptions.

---

8. Two Distinct Network Geometries

The passive dynamics are determined by L_G. The active minimum-dissipation metric is determined by L_G^+. Thus

\boxed{L_G \rightarrow \text{relaxation geometry}, \qquad L_G^+ \rightarrow \text{energetic control geometry}.}

The eigenvalues of L_G are 0=\lambda_1<\lambda_2\le\cdots\le\lambda_N. The corresponding pseudoinverse eigenvalues are 0,1/\lambda_2,\ldots,1/\lambda_N. The algebraic connectivity \lambda_2 determines the slowest passive relaxation and the largest energetic metric eigenvalue 1/\lambda_2; the spectral maximum \lambda_N corresponds to the smallest energetic metric eigenvalue 1/\lambda_N, identifying the most energetically efficient spectral direction.

---

9. Spectral Decoupling

Let L_G=U\Lambda U^\top, where \Lambda=\operatorname{diag}(0,\lambda_2,\ldots,\lambda_N). Define modal coordinates c(t)=U^\top x(t) and \tilde v(t)=U^\top v(t). The first eigenvector is \phi_1=\frac1{\sqrt N}\mathbf1, so c_1(t)=\frac{I(x)}{\sqrt N} is invariant. For k\ge2,

\dot c_k=-\lambda_kc_k+\tilde v_k.

The dissipation becomes P_{\min}=\sum_{k=2}^N \tilde v_k^2/\lambda_k. Therefore the total finite-time optimization separates into modal problems: W^*=\sum_{k=2}^N W_k^*.

---

10. Modal Minimum-Dissipation Problem

For each non-conserved mode, let a_k=c_k(0) and r_k=\sqrt{\epsilon_k}, with terminal sign chosen optimally: c_k(\tau)=\operatorname{sgn}(a_k)r_k. The exact minimum modal active dissipation is

\boxed{
W_k^*
=
\frac{(a_k^2+r_k^2)\cosh(\lambda_k\tau)-2|a_k|r_k}
{\sinh(\lambda_k\tau)}
+r_k^2-a_k^2
}

or equivalently

\boxed{
W_k^*
=
\frac{2\left(r_k e^{\lambda_k\tau}-|a_k|\right)^2}
{e^{2\lambda_k\tau}-1}.
}

The short-time expansion is

W_k^*
=
\frac{(|a_k|-r_k)^2}{\lambda_k\tau}
+
(r_k^2-a_k^2)
+
\frac{\lambda_k}{3}\left(a_k^2+|a_k|r_k+r_k^2\right)\tau
+
O(\tau^3).

Thus rapid reconciliation has a universal inverse-time divergence within this quadratic dissipation model: \tau\downarrow0\Longrightarrow W^*\uparrow\infty for any finite nonzero required state correction.

---

11. Two-Node Special Case

For two nodes, L_G=g\begin{pmatrix}1&-1\\-1&1\end{pmatrix} with eigenvalues \lambda_1=0, \lambda_2=2g. The only non-conserved mode is proportional to (1,-1). Defining \delta=\frac{x_1-x_2}{2}, the dynamics reduce to \dot\delta=-2g\delta+u, and the dissipation becomes W=\int_0^\tau \frac{u^2}{g}\,dt. For \delta(0)=\delta_0 and \delta(\tau)=\sqrt\epsilon, the exact minimum is

\boxed{
W^*
=
2\left[
\frac{(\delta_0^2+\epsilon)\cosh(2g\tau)-2\delta_0\sqrt\epsilon}
{\sinh(2g\tau)}
-(\delta_0^2-\epsilon)
\right].
}

This provides the elementary model from which the network theory generalizes.

---

12. Spectral Bounds

On the non-conserved subspace,

\frac1{\lambda_N}I_\perp \preceq L_G^+ \preceq \frac1{\lambda_2}I_\perp.

Therefore

\boxed{
\frac{\|v\|_2^2}{\lambda_N} \le v^\top L_G^+v \le \frac{\|v\|_2^2}{\lambda_2}.
}

Algebraic connectivity \lambda_2 determines the slowest passive relaxation: \tau_{\rm slow}\sim1/\lambda_2. It also corresponds to the largest energetic metric eigenvalue 1/\lambda_2. Thus the slowest global disagreement mode is simultaneously the most energetically expensive mode to actively control. Spectral maximum \lambda_N corresponds to the smallest energetic metric eigenvalue 1/\lambda_N, identifying the most energetically efficient spectral direction. This yields the central duality:

\boxed{
\begin{array}{ccc}
\text{Laplacian} &\longleftrightarrow& \text{Pseudoinverse}\\
L_G & & L_G^+\\
\lambda_k & & 1/\lambda_k\\
\text{relaxation rate} & & \text{control cost}
\end{array}
}

---

13. Thermodynamic Qualification: Active Control Cost vs. Total Dissipation

The functional \int_0^\tau v^\top L_G^+v\,dt is the minimum active control dissipation needed to generate the node correction v(t). It is not automatically the total physical dissipation of the network, because the passive term -L_Gx also corresponds to edge currents. The total edge current is u_{\rm tot}=u_{\rm passive}+u_{\rm active}, and the total edge dissipation is u_{\rm tot}^\top G_e^{-1}u_{\rm tot}, not simply v^\top L_G^+v. Under a linear irreversible constitutive law J_e=L_eF_e, the entropy production rate is T\dot S_i=J_e^\top L_e^{-1}J_e. Under the correspondence L_e=G_e, the edge-space quadratic form becomes T\dot S_i=u^\top G_e^{-1}u. The mathematical framework therefore provides a thermodynamic interpretation under specified physical assumptions rather than assuming that every quadratic control cost is automatically thermodynamic work.

---

14. The Spectral Water-Filling Principle

Theorem 2 (Optimal Modal Error Allocation). Consider the global reconciliation problem over time \tau:

\min_{\{\epsilon_k\ge0\}} \sum_{k=2}^N W_k^*(\lambda_k,\epsilon_k,\tau,c_{k,0})
\quad \text{subject to} \quad
\sum_{k=2}^N \epsilon_k \le \epsilon.

Let y_k=\sqrt{\epsilon_k}\ge0 and a_k=|c_{k,0}|\ge0. The modal work W_k^*(y_k) admits the exact quadratic form

\boxed{
W_k^*(y_k)=\frac{2\left(y_k-a_k e^{-\lambda_k\tau}\right)^2}{1-e^{-2\lambda_k\tau}}
}
\tag{14.1}

or equivalently, after expansion,

\boxed{
W_k^*(y_k)=\frac{2y_k^2}{1-e^{-2\lambda_k\tau}}
-\frac{2a_k y_k}{\sinh(\lambda_k\tau)}
+\frac{2a_k^{2}}{e^{2\lambda_k\tau}-1}.
}
\tag{14.2}

Two algebraic features of (14.1)–(14.2) must be noted:

· The overall prefactor is 2, not 4/\lambda_k. The factor 1/\lambda_k is already encoded in the modal control weight \tilde v_k^2/\lambda_k and cancels against the inverse Laplacian in the derivation; introducing an additional 1/\lambda_k double-counts the energetic metric.
· The cross term involves a_k=|c_{k,0}|, not c_{k,0}. Because y_k\ge0 by construction, a signed cross term would produce a linear function with negative slope for negative c_{k,0}, which is not the correct minimum-work functional.

Unconstrained passive minima. Setting \partial W_k^*/\partial y_k=0 yields the unconstrained minimizer

\boxed{y_k^{\rm pass}=a_k e^{-\lambda_k\tau},\qquad \epsilon_k^{\rm pass}=c_{k,0}^2 e^{-2\lambda_k\tau}.}
\tag{14.3}

This is precisely the uncontrolled passive relaxation of mode k under \dot c_k=-\lambda_kc_k. If \sum_{k=2}^N \epsilon_k^{\rm pass}\le\epsilon, no active control energy is required (W^*=0, corresponding to KKT multiplier \mu=0).

Constrained allocation. Forming the Lagrangian \mathcal L(y,\mu)=\sum_{k=2}^N W_k^*(y_k)+\mu(\sum_{k=2}^N y_k^2-\epsilon) with KKT multiplier \mu\ge0, stationarity gives

\frac{4\left(y_k-a_k e^{-\lambda_k\tau}\right)}{1-e^{-2\lambda_k\tau}}+2\mu y_k=0.

Solving for the optimal modal error radius,

\boxed{
y_k^*(\mu)=\frac{a_k\,e^{-\lambda_k\tau}}{1+\mu\,\alpha_k(\tau)},
\qquad
\alpha_k(\tau)=\frac{1-e^{-2\lambda_k\tau}}{2}>0.
}
\tag{14.4}

The multiplier \mu\ge0 is determined by \sum_{k=2}^N y_k^*(\mu)^2=\epsilon when the constraint is active, and is set to \mu=0 otherwise.

Remarks.

(i) The factor \alpha_k(\tau) is bounded: \alpha_k(\tau)\to\frac12 as \lambda_k\to\infty, and \alpha_k(\tau)\approx\lambda_k\tau as \lambda_k\tau\to0. In particular 0<\alpha_k(\tau)\le\frac12 for all \lambda_k,\tau\ge0. This is a qualitative departure from the naive scaling \alpha_k\sim\lambda_k/4 that would arise if the 1/\lambda_k metric were double-counted.

(ii) Equation (14.4) is well-defined when c_{k,0}=0; such modes give y_k^*=0 and require no control, and they drop out of the optimization without altering the multiplier structure.

(iii) The ratio

\frac{y_k^*}{y_k^{\rm pass}}=\frac{1}{1+\mu\,\alpha_k(\tau)}

measures the active suppression factor relative to the passive trajectory. It is bounded above by 1 and bounded below by 1/(1+\mu/2). Fast modes are suppressed at most by a factor 1/(1+\mu/2); slow modes are essentially unsuppressed (\alpha_k\to0).

Physical consequences. The expression (14.4) reveals how optimal non-equilibrium control distributes the terminal error budget across the graph spectrum.

Fast modes (\lambda_k\to\lambda_N, large \lambda_k). Here \alpha_k(\tau)\to\frac12, so the suppression ratio y_k^*/y_k^{\rm pass} saturates at 1/(1+\mu/2) regardless of how large \lambda_k becomes. The optimizer therefore suppresses fast modes relative to their passive trajectory by a bounded factor. This is not the dominant mechanism by which fast modes are rendered energetically negligible: that role is played by the exponentially small passive residual a_k e^{-\lambda_k\tau} itself. In other words, fast modes contribute negligibly to the terminal error budget primarily because passive relaxation has already driven them close to zero, and only secondarily because the optimizer spends control authority on them.

Slow modes (\lambda_k\to\lambda_2, small \lambda_k). Here \alpha_k(\tau)\approx\lambda_k\tau\ll1, so the denominator 1+\mu\alpha_k(\tau)\approx1 and y_k^*\approx a_k e^{-\lambda_k\tau}=y_k^{\rm pass}. The optimizer cannot reduce slow-mode error without incurring prohibitive energetic cost, because the control weight 1/\lambda_k is largest in exactly these directions. The error budget \epsilon is therefore concentrated almost entirely into the slow modes.

\boxed{\text{Optimal Reconciliation Strategy: Fast modes are suppressed at most by the bounded factor }1/(1+\mu/2)\text{, while the error budget }\epsilon\text{ is absorbed in the slow modes.}}

This gives a clean spectral allocation law: modes with larger \lambda_k are cheaper per unit of state correction (control weight 1/\lambda_k) and are suppressed relative to their passive trajectory by the largest factor; modes with smaller \lambda_k are more expensive and retain most of their residual error. The qualitative conclusion of the water-filling picture survives; the quantitative scaling changes from a linear-in-\lambda_k suppression factor to a bounded one, with the exponential decay e^{-\lambda_k\tau} of the fast-mode passive residuals responsible for the bulk of the error concentration in slow modes.

---

15. Beyond Exact Conservation

The present model assumes an exact invariant I(x)=\mathbf1^\top x. Many biological and adaptive systems instead preserve functionality only approximately. A minimal extension introduces a mean-mode control v_1\in\operatorname{span}\{\mathbf1\} with its own quadratic cost \gamma v_1^2, replacing exact conservation by D(I(x_0),I(x_\tau))\le\epsilon. The invariant-reconciliation problem then becomes a joint optimization over v_1 and v_\perp. Edge-space control alone cannot change the global invariant because v=Bu automatically satisfies \mathbf1^\top v=0; external source/sink terms or a separate mean-mode cost are required.

---

16. Research Hypotheses

H1 — Spectral bottleneck hypothesis. For otherwise comparable networks, decreasing \lambda_2(L_G) increases the difficulty of rapid global reconciliation.

H2 — Finite-time dissipation hypothesis. For fixed G, x_0, and \epsilon, W^*(G,\epsilon,\tau) diverges asymptotically as 1/\tau when \tau\to0.

H3 — Consistency–dissipation frontier. For fixed topology and initial state, \epsilon_1<\epsilon_2 implies W^*(G,\epsilon_1,\tau)\ge W^*(G,\epsilon_2,\tau).

H4 — Spectral mode allocation. When the terminal error budget is optimized rather than prescribed mode-by-mode, the optimal allocation \{\epsilon_k^*\} follows the water-filling law

y_k^*(\mu)=\frac{|c_{k,0}|e^{-\lambda_k\tau}}{1+\mu\,\alpha_k(\tau)},
\qquad
\alpha_k(\tau)=\frac{1-e^{-2\lambda_k\tau}}{2},

with the multiplier \mu\ge0 determined by \sum_{k=2}^N y_k^*(\mu)^2=\epsilon when the constraint is active. The suppression ratio y_k^*/y_k^{\rm pass}=1/[1+\mu\alpha_k(\tau)] is bounded above by 1 and bounded below by 1/(1+\mu/2), so fast modes are suppressed relative to their passive trajectory by at most the factor 1/(1+\mu/2), while slow modes are essentially unsuppressed. The error budget concentrates in slow modes primarily through the exponential decay of fast-mode passive residuals and secondarily through bounded active suppression.

---

17. Limitations

First, the model is linear. Real transport networks may exhibit nonlinear conductance, saturation, threshold behavior, or state-dependent topology. Second, the graph is assumed connected. Disconnected graphs possess multiple zero Laplacian modes and therefore multiple independently conserved components. Third, the dissipation functional assumes a quadratic linear-response regime. Fourth, the identification of mathematical dissipation with physical thermodynamic work requires a constitutive physical realization. Fifth, the framework currently treats the graph topology as fixed. Adaptive or evolving networks require G=G(t,x) and therefore a substantially more complicated theory. Sixth, the water-filling allocation assumes a single global KKT multiplier \mu. Modes with c_{k,0}=0 give y_k^*=0 and drop out of the optimization without requiring modification of the multiplier structure; but if the constraint set were modified so that some modes are explicitly forced to zero allocation while others are active, the KKT complementarity conditions would need to be re-derived. Seventh, because \alpha_k(\tau) is bounded, the constraint \sum y_k^{*2}=\epsilon cannot be satisfied by fast-mode suppression alone in the regime where slow-mode passive residuals already exceed \epsilon; in that regime the multiplier \mu grows without bound and the solution approaches the minimum-cost projection onto the constraint set.

---

18. Conclusion

We have formulated distributed invariant reconciliation as a finite-time minimum-dissipation control problem on a weighted graph. The central dynamical equation is \dot x=-L_Gx+v, while the minimum instantaneous dissipation required to implement a node-space correction is

\boxed{P_{\min}(v)=v^\top L_G^+v.}

This establishes a fundamental distinction between two geometries induced by the same network topology:

\boxed{L_G \rightarrow \text{passive relaxation geometry}, \qquad L_G^+ \rightarrow \text{active energetic geometry}.}

Spectral decomposition produces independent reconciliation modes \dot c_k=-\lambda_kc_k+\tilde v_k with energetic weights 1/\lambda_k. The algebraic connectivity \lambda_2 controls the slowest passive relaxation mode and the greatest energetic susceptibility, while \lambda_N defines the most energetically favorable spectral direction.

The resulting optimization,

\boxed{
W^*(G,\epsilon,\tau,x_0)
=
\inf_v\left\{
\int_0^\tau v^\top L_G^+v\,dt :
\dot x=-L_Gx+v,\;
\|P_\perp x(\tau)\|^2\le\epsilon
\right\},
}

admits a closed-form modal solution and a spectral water-filling allocation law

y_k^*(\mu)=\frac{|c_{k,0}|e^{-\lambda_k\tau}}{1+\mu\,\alpha_k(\tau)},
\qquad
\alpha_k(\tau)=\frac{1-e^{-2\lambda_k\tau}}{2},

for the terminal error budget. The central conceptual result is that network topology induces both a relaxation geometry and an energetic control geometry, related through the Laplacian and its pseudoinverse. The optimal reconciliation strategy concentrates the residual error budget in the slow modes, because slow modes are energetically expensive to control; fast modes are already exponentially close to zero under passive relaxation, and active suppression of fast modes is bounded by the factor 1/(1+\mu/2). This may reveal a deeper relationship between topology, finite-time control, and the geometry of invariant preservation.

Appendices :


Appendix A. Proof of the Control Metric Identity (Theorem 1)

Setup. Let G=(V,E) be connected, B\in\mathbb R^{N\times M} an oriented incidence matrix, G_e=\operatorname{diag}(g_1,\dots,g_M) with g_e>0, and L_G=BG_eB^\top. Recall that \operatorname{Range}(B)=\operatorname{Range}(L_G)=\mathbf 1^\perp.

Statement. For every v\in\mathbf 1^\perp,

\min_{u\in\mathbb R^M:\,Bu=v} u^\top G_e^{-1}u = v^\top L_G^+v.

Proof. Consider the Lagrangian

\mathcal L(u,\gamma)=u^\top G_e^{-1}u+\gamma^\top(v-Bu),\qquad u\in\mathbb R^M,\ \gamma\in\mathbb R^N.

The functional P(u)=u^\top G_e^{-1}u is strictly convex on \mathbb R^M because G_e^{-1}\succ 0, and the feasible set \{u:Bu=v\} is an affine subspace, hence convex. Therefore any feasible stationary point of \mathcal L is the unique global minimizer.

Stationarity. Setting \nabla_u\mathcal L=0:

2G_e^{-1}u-B^\top\gamma=0\quad\Longrightarrow\quad u=\tfrac12 G_eB^\top\gamma.

Feasibility. Substituting u into Bu=v:

\tfrac12 BG_eB^\top\gamma=v\quad\Longrightarrow\quad \tfrac12 L_G\gamma=v.

Since v\in\mathbf 1^\perp=\operatorname{Range}(L_G), the minimum-norm solution of L_G\gamma=2v is \gamma^\star=2L_G^+v (the general solution is \gamma^\star+\alpha\mathbf 1, but adding \alpha\mathbf 1 leaves u unchanged because B^\top\mathbf 1=0). Hence

u^\star=G_eB^\top L_G^+v.

Value at the optimum. Substituting u^\star and using L_G=BG_eB^\top:

\begin{aligned}
P(u^\star)
&=u^{\star\top}G_e^{-1}u^\star
=(G_eB^\top L_G^+v)^\top G_e^{-1}(G_eB^\top L_G^+v)\\
&=v^\top L_G^+BG_eB^\top L_G^+v
=v^\top L_G^+L_GL_G^+v.
\end{aligned}

The Moore–Penrose identity L_G^+L_GL_G^+=L_G^+ (valid for symmetric L_G) gives

P(u^\star)=v^\top L_G^+v.

Uniqueness follows from strict convexity. \square

Remark (thermodynamic reading). Under the linear irreversible constitutive law J_e=L_eF_e with L_e=G_e, the entropy production rate is T\dot S_i=J_e^\top L_e^{-1}J_e=u^\top G_e^{-1}u. The identity above then expresses the minimum entropy production required to sustain a prescribed node-space correction v.

