Appendix C. Modal Minimum-Work Formula

Setup. Fix a mode k\ge2. Let a_k=c_k(0), \lambda=\lambda_k>0, and let r_k\ge0 be the prescribed terminal magnitude, |c_k(\tau)|=r_k. The modal control cost is

W_k=\frac{1}{\lambda}\int_0^\tau \tilde v_k(t)^2\,dt,\qquad \dot c_k=-\lambda c_k+\tilde v_k.

Proposition C.1. The optimal terminal sign is \operatorname{sgn}(c_k(\tau))=\operatorname{sgn}(a_k) whenever a_k\neq 0, and the minimum modal work is

\boxed{
W_k^\star=\frac{2\left(r_k e^{\lambda\tau}-|a_k|\right)^2}{e^{2\lambda\tau}-1}.
}

Proof.

Step 1: reduction to a variational problem. Substitute \tilde v_k=\dot c_k+\lambda c_k:

W_k=\frac{1}{\lambda}\int_0^\tau\bigl(\dot c_k+\lambda c_k\bigr)^2dt.

Step 2: Euler–Lagrange equation. Let g(t)=\dot c_k(t)+\lambda c_k(t). The Lagrangian density is L(c,\dot c)=(\dot c+\lambda c)^2. Then

\frac{\partial L}{\partial \dot c}=2(\dot c+\lambda c)=2g,\qquad \frac{\partial L}{\partial c}=2\lambda(\dot c+\lambda c)=2\lambda g.

The Euler–Lagrange equation \frac{d}{dt}\frac{\partial L}{\partial \dot c}=\frac{\partial L}{\partial c} reads

2\dot g=2\lambda g\quad\Longrightarrow\quad \dot g=\lambda g\quad\Longrightarrow\quad g(t)=g_0e^{\lambda t}.

Step 3: solve the ODE. With g(t)=g_0e^{\lambda t}, the equation \dot c_k+\lambda c_k=g_0e^{\lambda t} has integrating factor e^{\lambda t}:

\frac{d}{dt}\!\left(c_ke^{\lambda t}\right)=g_0e^{2\lambda t}\ \Longrightarrow\ c_ke^{\lambda t}=\frac{g_0}{2\lambda}e^{2\lambda t}+C.

Hence

c_k(t)=\frac{g_0}{2\lambda}e^{\lambda t}+Ce^{-\lambda t}.

Step 4: impose boundary conditions. Let c_k(0)=a_k, c_k(\tau)=b_k. Then

a_k=\frac{g_0}{2\lambda}+C,\qquad b_k=\frac{g_0}{2\lambda}e^{\lambda\tau}+Ce^{-\lambda\tau}.

Eliminating C=a_k-\frac{g_0}{2\lambda}:

b_k=\frac{g_0}{2\lambda}\bigl(e^{\lambda\tau}-e^{-\lambda\tau}\bigr)+a_ke^{-\lambda\tau}
=\frac{g_0}{\lambda}\sinh(\lambda\tau)+a_ke^{-\lambda\tau}.

Therefore

g_0=\frac{\lambda\bigl(b_k-a_ke^{-\lambda\tau}\bigr)}{\sinh(\lambda\tau)}.

Step 5: evaluate the work. Since g(t)=g_0e^{\lambda t},

W_k=\frac{1}{\lambda}\int_0^\tau g_0^2e^{2\lambda t}\,dt
=\frac{g_0^2}{\lambda}\cdot\frac{e^{2\lambda\tau}-1}{2\lambda}
=\frac{g_0^2(e^{2\lambda\tau}-1)}{2\lambda^2}.

Substitute g_0^2=\lambda^2(b_k-a_ke^{-\lambda\tau})^2/\sinh^2(\lambda\tau):

W_k=\frac{(b_k-a_ke^{-\lambda\tau})^2(e^{2\lambda\tau}-1)}{2\sinh^2(\lambda\tau)}.

Use e^{2\lambda\tau}-1=2e^{\lambda\tau}\sinh(\lambda\tau):

W_k=\frac{(b_k-a_ke^{-\lambda\tau})^2e^{\lambda\tau}}{\sinh(\lambda\tau)}
=\frac{2\bigl(b_ke^{\lambda\tau}-a_k\bigr)^2}{e^{2\lambda\tau}-1}.

Step 6: optimal terminal sign. We are free to choose b_k=\pm r_k. The expression 2(b_ke^{\lambda\tau}-a_k)^2/(e^{2\lambda\tau}-1) is minimized over b_k\in\{\pm r_k\} by choosing b_k=\operatorname{sgn}(a_k)r_k whenever a_k\neq 0, which yields

\bigl(b_ke^{\lambda\tau}-a_k\bigr)^2=\bigl(r_ke^{\lambda\tau}-|a_k|\bigr)^2.

If a_k=0, both signs give the same value. \square

Corollary C.2 (equivalent hyperbolic form). The same minimum work admits

W_k^\star=\frac{(a_k^2+r_k^2)\cosh(\lambda\tau)-2|a_k|r_k}{\sinh(\lambda\tau)}+r_k^2-a_k^2.

Proof. Expand the squared numerator in Proposition C.1:

2\bigl(r_ke^{\lambda\tau}-|a_k|\bigr)^2
=2r_k^2e^{2\lambda\tau}-4|a_k|r_ke^{\lambda\tau}+2a_k^2.

Divide numerator and denominator by e^{\lambda\tau}:

W_k^\star
=\frac{2r_k^2e^{\lambda\tau}-4|a_k|r_k+2a_k^2e^{-\lambda\tau}}{e^{\lambda\tau}-e^{-\lambda\tau}}
=\frac{2r_k^2e^{\lambda\tau}-4|a_k|r_k+2a_k^2e^{-\lambda\tau}}{2\sinh(\lambda\tau)}.

Now 2r_k^2e^{\lambda\tau}+2a_k^2e^{-\lambda\tau}=(a_k^2+r_k^2)(e^{\lambda\tau}+e^{-\lambda\tau})+(r_k^2-a_k^2)(e^{\lambda\tau}-e^{-\lambda\tau}), so

W_k^\star
=\frac{(a_k^2+r_k^2)\cosh(\lambda\tau)-2|a_k|r_k+(r_k^2-a_k^2)\sinh(\lambda\tau)}{\sinh(\lambda\tau)}
=\frac{(a_k^2+r_k^2)\cosh(\lambda\tau)-2|a_k|r_k}{\sinh(\lambda\tau)}+r_k^2-a_k^2.

\square

