Appendix J. Existence, Uniqueness, and Complementary Slackness for the Water-Filling Problem

Proposition J.1. The optimization

\min_{y\ge0}\ \sum_{k=2}^N \beta_k(y_k-b_k)^2\quad\text{subject to}\quad \sum_{k=2}^N y_k^2\le\epsilon

with \beta_k>0, b_k\ge0, \epsilon>0 has a unique minimizer, characterized by either

· (passive case) \sum_kb_k^2\le\epsilon and y_k=b_k for all k; or
· (active case) \sum_kb_k^2>\epsilon, \mu>0, and y_k=\beta_kb_k/(\beta_k+\mu) with \sum_k y_k^2=\epsilon.

Proof.

Existence. The feasible set is closed, bounded (since y_k^2\le\epsilon), and nonempty (the origin is feasible because \epsilon>0). The objective is continuous and strictly convex (sum of strictly convex quadratics), so a unique minimizer exists.

Uniqueness. Strict convexity of the objective on the convex feasible set implies uniqueness.

KKT characterization. Because the constraint is convex and Slater's condition holds (origin is interior when \epsilon>0), the KKT conditions are necessary and sufficient for optimality: there is \mu\ge0 such that

2\beta_k(y_k-b_k)+2\mu y_k=0,\quad k=2,\dots,N,\qquad
\mu\ge0,\qquad
\sum_k y_k^2\le\epsilon,\qquad
\mu\Bigl(\sum_k y_k^2-\epsilon\Bigr)=0.

If the constraint is inactive, \mu=0 and y_k=b_k; consistency requires \sum_kb_k^2<\epsilon. If active, \mu>0, \sum_k y_k^2=\epsilon, and y_k=\beta_kb_k/(\beta_k+\mu) as in Theorem E.3. \square

Proposition J.2 (monotonicity in \epsilon). The optimal value W^\star(\epsilon)=\sum_k W_k^\star(y_k^\star(\epsilon)) is nonincreasing in \epsilon.

Proof. If \epsilon_1<\epsilon_2, any y feasible for \epsilon_1 is feasible for \epsilon_2, so the feasible set for \epsilon_2 contains that for \epsilon_1, and the minimum over a larger set is no larger. \square

Corollary J.3 (Research hypothesis H3). The consistency–dissipation frontier is monotone: W^\star(\epsilon) is nonincreasing in \epsilon, with equality iff both constraints admit the same optimal y.

Proof. Immediate from Proposition J.2.

