F. Water-Filling and Power Allocation

F1. Lozano, A., Tulino, A. M., & Verdú, S. (2006). Optimum power allocation for parallel Gaussian channels with arbitrary input distributions. IEEE Transactions on Information Theory, 52(7), 3033–3051. 
Annotation. Gives the power allocation policy that maximizes mutual information over parallel channels with arbitrary input distributions, introducing the “mercury/waterfilling” generalization. The classical water-filling principle is the direct ancestor of the spectral water-filling law in Theorem 2.

F2. Cover, T. M., & Thomas, J. A. (2006). Elements of Information Theory (2nd ed.). John Wiley & Sons. 
Annotation. Standard reference for the water-filling solution to power allocation over parallel Gaussian channels. The mathematical structure—maximizing a sum of concave functions subject to a total resource constraint—is isomorphic to the modal error allocation problem.

F3. Mercury/waterfilling: Optimum power allocation with arbitrary input constellations. (2005). IEEE. 
Annotation. Extends water-filling to discrete constellations. Illustrates the generality of the water-filling principle beyond Gaussian inputs.

F4. Robust Power Allocation for Parallel Gaussian Channels With Approximately Gaussian Input Distributions. (2020). IEEE. 
Annotation. Proposes a robust water-filling policy under KL-divergence uncertainty. Relevant for extensions of the present framework to model uncertainty.

F5. Unified Interference-Aware Water-Filling for QoS-Constrained Communication, Sensing, and JRC. (2025). arXiv:2506.01400. 
Annotation. Solves a non-convex water-filling problem via convex subproblems and KKT conditions. Provides a modern example of KKT-based water-filling, supporting the derivation in Theorem 2.

